# vpn-tools.conf — shared configuration for openvpn-install.sh and vpn-status.py
#
# Plain KEY=VALUE lines (no spaces around '='), sourceable directly by bash
# and parsed by the Python tools with a simple split. Values are not quoted.
#
# This file is expected at /etc/openvpn/vpn-tools.conf by default; both
# tools fall back to sane defaults if the file is missing entirely, so a
# fresh install works out of the box. Copy this file there and uncomment
# only what you actually want to override.

# Public IP or hostname clients connect to (informational here; the actual
# value used by generated .ovpn files still comes from client-common.txt)
#SERVER_PUBLIC_IP=203.0.113.10
#SERVER_PORT=1194
#SERVER_PROTO=udp

# systemd unit managing the OpenVPN server process
#SERVICE_NAME=openvpn-server@server.service

# Core OpenVPN server paths
#OPENVPN_DIR=/etc/openvpn/server
#EASYRSA_DIR=/etc/openvpn/server/easy-rsa
#DB_FILE=/etc/openvpn/server/openvpn_db.txt
#CLIENT_COMMON_FILE=/etc/openvpn/server/client-common.txt

# Logs
#STATUS_LOG=/var/log/openvpn/openvpn-status.log
#CONN_LOG=/etc/openvpn/server/openvpn.log

# --- Per-client restrictions (country / city / ASN / IP / OS / bandwidth) ---
# See README.md's "Per-client restrictions" section for the full picture.
# Enforced by the client-connect/client-disconnect scripts on the OpenVPN
# host (host-scripts/ in this repo -- installed to /etc/openvpn/server/,
# NOT part of the web app's Docker image); read/written by
# client_policy_cli.py (this script's --set-country/--set-os/
# --set-bandwidth/--get-policy subcommands -- note city/ASN/IP have no CLI
# equivalent yet, use the web UI or edit client_policy.json directly) and
# by the web app directly (it bind-mounts /etc/openvpn rw, see
# docker-compose.yml).
#CLIENT_POLICY_FILE=/etc/openvpn/server/policy/client_policy.json
#CLIENT_USAGE_FILE=/etc/openvpn/server/policy/client_usage.json

# MaxMind GeoLite2 databases -- Country/City/ASN are three independent
# editions/files, each used only by its matching restriction check and
# never even opened for a client with no restriction of that kind
# configured (see host-scripts/policy_lib.py's geoip_lookup_country/
# geoip_lookup_city/geoip_lookup_asn docstrings for the fail-safe design).
# IP address restriction needs none of these (pure ipaddress comparison).
# Sign up for a free license key at
# https://www.maxmind.com/en/geolite2/signup and set MAXMIND_LICENSE_KEY
# to enable country/city/ASN restrictions; leave it blank to keep all
# three disabled (geoip-update.sh exits cleanly, without erroring, when
# unset). geoip-update.sh fetches all three editions on each run once a
# key is set -- there's no way to enable just one.
#MAXMIND_LICENSE_KEY=
#MAXMIND_DB_PATH=/etc/openvpn/server/GeoLite2-Country.mmdb
#MAXMIND_CITY_DB_PATH=/etc/openvpn/server/GeoLite2-City.mmdb
#MAXMIND_ASN_DB_PATH=/etc/openvpn/server/GeoLite2-ASN.mmdb

# Where newly-generated .ovpn client files are delivered, and to whom they
# should be chown'd (so they can be retrieved without needing root).
#
# Left commented out by default: openvpn-install.sh auto-detects this from
# whoever actually ran `sudo` to invoke it ($SUDO_USER and their home dir),
# falling back to the first regular human account on the box, then root --
# it does NOT assume a specific distro's default account name (e.g.
# "ubuntu"), since the community running this could be on Debian, a
# different cloud image, or a custom setup. Only set these explicitly if
# you want delivery to go to an account OTHER than whoever ran the install.
#OVPN_OUTPUT_DIR=/home/someuser
#OVPN_OUTPUT_OWNER=someuser:someuser
OVPN_OUTPUT_MODE=600

# --- IMPORTANT if you run the web app (app/) in Docker ---
# The app container only bind-mounts /etc/openvpn (rw) and /var/log/openvpn
# (ro) from the host -- see app/docker-compose.yml. A default/home-directory
# OVPN_OUTPUT_DIR (e.g. /home/ubuntu, /root) is NOT visible inside that
# container: files really do land there on the host when a human runs
# openvpn-install.sh directly, but the containerized app's own --show-ovpn/
# --add-user calls resolve that same path inside the container's filesystem,
# where it's an unrelated, normally-empty directory -- so the app reports
# "no .ovpn file found" even though the file genuinely exists on the host.
# If you're running the app in Docker, set OVPN_OUTPUT_DIR to a path under
# /etc/openvpn instead, e.g.:
#OVPN_OUTPUT_DIR=/etc/openvpn/client
#OVPN_OUTPUT_OWNER=root:root
